{"author":{"id":"89da5fec8eff6d69dbcdd68f81be7e9163b16f34b17e978ecf3554487379f453"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1773338942,"nonce":"LOc0wyb1TkUQbPxOerQGBu6ZVwA=","title":"git-bug: no mechanism to record resolving commit/merge when closing an issue","message":"When closing an issue via `git-bug bug status close \u003cid\u003e`, there is no field to attach the resolving commit hash or merge ID. This means the link between \"issue closed\" and \"what code closed it\" lives only in the commit message Bug: trailer (if present) or in a manually-written comment.\n\n**Impact:** Issues closed by auth-merge auto-close (via Bug: trailers) lose the specific merge commit reference unless the comment is added manually. Retroactively finding what closed an issue requires grepping git log for the issue ID prefix.\n\n**Proposed fix:** Require (or at minimum strongly encourage) a resolving commit reference when closing an issue — either:\n1. A `--resolve-commit \u003csha\u003e` flag added to git-bug close (upstream feature request)\n2. A local convention: always add a comment with the merge SHA before closing\n3. A wrapper script `bug-close` that requires a SHA and writes the comment atomically\n\nUntil (1) is available upstream, (3) is the pragmatic path.\n\nThis issue is part of the auth-merge reliability partition — discovered during issue triage.","files":null}]}